#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555964 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=- x - 3 y + 2 z$ and $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+(t^{2})\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- x - 3 y + 2 z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- x - 3 y + 2 z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(- x - 3 y + 2 z)\\,k \\\\  & =(-1)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y=t^{2},$ and $z=t^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(-1)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((-1)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(2)\\,k)\\cdot ((1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (1)+(-3)\\cdot (2 t)+(2)\\cdot (2 t) \\\\  & =(-1)+(- 6 t)+(4 t) \\\\  & =- (2 t + 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555965 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=- 3 x - 2 y - 3 z$ and $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+(t^{2})\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 3 x - 2 y - 3 z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 3 x - 2 y - 3 z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(- 3 x - 2 y - 3 z)\\,k \\\\  & =(-3)\\,i+(-2)\\,j+(-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y=t^{2},$ and $z=t^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(-3)\\,i+(-2)\\,j+(-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((-3)\\,i+(-2)\\,j+(-3)\\,k)\\cdot ((1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (1)+(-2)\\cdot (2 t)+(-3)\\cdot (2 t) \\\\  & =(-3)+(- 4 t)+(- 6 t) \\\\  & =- (10 t + 3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555975 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x - 3 y - 2 z$ and $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+(t)\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x - 3 y - 2 z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x - 3 y - 2 z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x - 3 y - 2 z)\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(-2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y=t^{2},$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(1)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(-2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((1)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(-2)\\,k)\\cdot ((1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(1)\\,k) \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot (1)+(-3)\\cdot (2 t)+(-2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(1)+(- 6 t)+(-2) \\\\  & =- (6 t + 1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555990 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=4\\left(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right)$ and $r(t)={(t + 1)}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\,j+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4\\left(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4\\left(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4\\left(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,k \\\\  & =(4\\left(-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\right))\\,i+(4\\left(+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,j+(4\\left(+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={(t + 1)}^{2},y={e}^{(t + 1)},$ and $z={e}^{(t + 1)}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(4\\left(-{e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,i+(4\\left(+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,j+(4\\left(+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({(t + 1)}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{(t + 1)})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{(t + 1)})\\,k \\\\  & =(2(t + 1))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t + 1)})\\,j+(+{e}^{(t + 1)})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((4\\left(-{e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,i+(4\\left(+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,j+(4\\left(+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right))\\,k)\\cdot ((2(t + 1))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t + 1)})\\,j+(+{e}^{(t + 1)})\\,k) \\\\  & =4\\left(-2(t + 1){e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t + 1)}+{e}^{(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t + 1)}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(-2(t + 1){e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})+2{e}^{2(t + 1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})\\right) \\\\  & =4\\left(2{e}^{2(t + 1)}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})-(t + 1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2}))\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555998 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)={(t - 2)}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\,j+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,k \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={(t - 2)}^{2},y={e}^{(t - 2)},$ and $z={e}^{(t - 2)}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(-{e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,j+(+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({(t - 2)}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{(t - 2)})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{(t - 2)})\\,k \\\\  & =(2(t - 2))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t - 2)})\\,j+(+{e}^{(t - 2)})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((-{e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,j+(+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))\\,k)\\cdot ((2(t - 2))\\,i+(+{e}^{(t - 2)})\\,j+(+{e}^{(t - 2)})\\,k) \\\\  & =-2(t - 2){e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t - 2)}+{e}^{(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{(t - 2)} \\\\  & =-2(t - 2){e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})+2{e}^{2(t - 2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2(t - 2)}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})-(t - 2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555999 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=2 x - 3 y + 2 z$ and $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+(t^{2})\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x - 3 y + 2 z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x - 3 y + 2 z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2 x - 3 y + 2 z)\\,k \\\\  & =(2)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y=t^{2},$ and $z=t^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2)\\,i+(-3)\\,j+(2)\\,k)\\cdot ((1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(2 t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot (1)+(-3)\\cdot (2 t)+(2)\\cdot (2 t) \\\\  & =(2)+(- 6 t)+(4 t) \\\\  & =2 \\left(1 - t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556006 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x + y - 2 z$ and $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(t^{2})\\,j+(t)\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y - 2 z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y - 2 z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x + y - 2 z)\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(1)\\,j+(-2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y=t^{2},$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(1)\\,i+(1)\\,j+(-2)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(1)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((1)\\,i+(1)\\,j+(-2)\\,k)\\cdot ((1)\\,i+(2 t)\\,j+(1)\\,k) \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot (1)+(1)\\cdot (2 t)+(-2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =(1)+(2 t)+(-2) \\\\  & =2 t - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556011 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{x^{2}}{25}+\\frac{y^{2}}{100}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{x^{2}}{25}+\\frac{y^{2}}{100}=1$ in the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,10 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,\\quad t\\in[0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x^2}{25}+\\frac{y^2}{100}&=\\frac{(5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{25}+\\frac{(10 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{100}\\\\&=\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nf(t,z)=⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,10 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and $z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $10 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556012 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{x^{2}}{144}+\\frac{y^{2}}{4}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{x^{2}}{144}+\\frac{y^{2}}{4}=1$ in the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,\\quad t\\in[0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x^2}{144}+\\frac{y^2}{4}&=\\frac{(12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{144}+\\frac{(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{4}\\\\&=\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nf(t,z)=⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and $z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556013 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨9 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{x^{2}}{81}+\\frac{y^{2}}{49}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{x^{2}}{81}+\\frac{y^{2}}{49}=1$ in the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨9 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,7 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,\\quad t\\in[0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x^2}{81}+\\frac{y^2}{49}&=\\frac{(9 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{81}+\\frac{(7 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{49}\\\\&=\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nf(t,z)=⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨9 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,7 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and $z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $7 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556015 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{x^{2}}{36}+\\frac{y^{2}}{144}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{x^{2}}{36}+\\frac{y^{2}}{144}=1$ in the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,\\quad t\\in[0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x^2}{36}+\\frac{y^2}{144}&=\\frac{(6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{36}+\\frac{(12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{144}\\\\&=\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nf(t,z)=⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and $z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556023 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $4x^{2}+y^{2}=144.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $4x^{2}+y^{2}=144$, or equivalently $\\frac{x^{2}}{36}+\\frac{y^{2}}{144}=1$, in the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,\\quad t\\in[0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x^2}{36}+\\frac{y^2}{144}&=\\frac{(6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{36}+\\frac{(12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{144}\\\\&=\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nf(t,z)=⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and $z\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556045 :question/problem "$$\nf(t,y)=⟨4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{x^{2}}{16}+\\frac{z^{2}}{64}=1.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{x^{2}}{16}+\\frac{z^{2}}{64}=1$ in the $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,z⟩=⟨4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩,\\quad t\\in[0,2\\pi),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{z^2}{64}&=\\frac{(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{16}+\\frac{(8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{64}\\\\&=\\cos^2 t+\\sin^2 t\\\\&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nf(t,y)=⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\,y,\\,8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and $y\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556057 :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9343557caba8d0816ce08f755e124d1648780523145cd6864d3718550432014.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest tenth, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The marked angles are rounded to the precision shown; calculate the area estimate using the marked values." :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/59/59dd651e6e27665841463b1d6f8f8409801ca6bd5f15428e2cb5cf65328a2528.png)\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 21.1\\cdot 22.1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{56.4}^{∘} \\\\  & =233.155\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{56.4}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈194.199\\,8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 24.2\\cdot 16.7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{56.4}^{∘} \\\\  & =202.07\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{56.4}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈168.308\\,4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & ≈194.199\\,8+168.308\\,4 \\\\  & ≈362.508\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest tenth, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $362.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556065 :question/problem "![](images/13/131fbdc1d3e47edd13257d415ee888c2148ccb3716bfd97ddaaa16efaa56db15.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to the **nearest tenth**, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is{{field-1}}.\n\nEnter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point if needed.\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The marked angles are rounded to the precision shown; calculate the area estimate using the marked values." :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/1f/1f848c8c4766a074b4c2b8e7ecc1e71d23e9c2c1c89158b8988ab36b53879f61.png)\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 13.6\\cdot 10\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{87.3}^{∘} \\\\  & =68\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{87.3}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈67.924\\,5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8.7\\cdot 19.9\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{54.8}^{∘} \\\\  & =86.565\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{54.8}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈70.736\\,1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & ≈67.924\\,5+70.736\\,1 \\\\  & ≈138.660\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the **nearest tenth**, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $138.7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556068 :question/problem "![](images/44/44c617ba56886e7efe82d2b5624cb65effb54978ba810623990860d154830c40.png)\n\nRounded to the **nearest integer**, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD$ is{{field-1}}.\n\nEnter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point if needed.\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The dashed diagonal divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles." :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/44/44c617ba56886e7efe82d2b5624cb65effb54978ba810623990860d154830c40.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACB} & =\\frac{1}{2}(6)(17)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =51\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈44.167,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 44.167 \\\\  & ≈88.334.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the **nearest integer**, the area of the parallelogram is $88.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556073 :question/problem "![](images/71/71a43ad32d3566fba8505542947532e9750a8d1289997b4f8f03e28ca313299f.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest tenth, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The marked angles are rounded to the precision shown; calculate the area estimate using the marked values." :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/89/8924aebb92b72747f67bc2e0c0a604dad541485a714bdcb1ee6da9f1c5f51310.png)\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9.4\\cdot 27.1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{35.2}^{∘} \\\\  & =127.37\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{35.2}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈73.420\\,2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 13.9\\cdot 9.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{117.8}^{∘} \\\\  & =66.025\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{117.8}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈58.404\\,5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & ≈73.420\\,2+58.404\\,5 \\\\  & ≈131.824\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest tenth, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $131.8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556077 :question/problem "![](images/67/67555c7ecb4ca726c4153c7b4e6bcd377f06534bb42cbdc5b696b697788847c4.png)\n\nFind, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD.$\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The dashed diagonal divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles." :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/67/67555c7ecb4ca726c4153c7b4e6bcd377f06534bb42cbdc5b696b697788847c4.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACB} & =\\frac{1}{2}(18)(12)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =108\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈93.531,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 93.531 \\\\  & ≈187.062.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $187.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556082 :question/problem "![](images/58/589b9b43c038ffb4a4dafac8e8f42f936478230f3721e4386a4c0a061e62103b.png)\n\nFind, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD.$\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The dashed diagonal divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles." :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/58/589b9b43c038ffb4a4dafac8e8f42f936478230f3721e4386a4c0a061e62103b.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACB} & =\\frac{1}{2}(14)(9)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =63\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈54.560,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 54.560 \\\\  & ≈109.120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $109.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556083 :question/problem "![](images/38/3844c3042ed7433f478f732aada7bfc73faa3f07608bf25778d4d87dc0a14968.png)\n\nFind, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD.$\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The dashed diagonal divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles." :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/38/3844c3042ed7433f478f732aada7bfc73faa3f07608bf25778d4d87dc0a14968.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACB} & =\\frac{1}{2}(15)(5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =37.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈32.476,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 32.476 \\\\  & ≈64.952.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $65.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556085 :question/problem "![](images/24/245045911890f7cbd669087241954a3b100c6d15ec1bf511e4557d6e89c47db9.png)\n\nRounded to the **nearest integer**, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD$ is{{field-1}}.\n\nEnter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point if needed.\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The dashed diagonal divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles." :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/24/245045911890f7cbd669087241954a3b100c6d15ec1bf511e4557d6e89c47db9.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACB} & =\\frac{1}{2}(11)(17)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =93.5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈80.973,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 80.973 \\\\  & ≈161.946.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the **nearest integer**, the area of the parallelogram is $162.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556094 :question/problem "![](images/a9/a941692b5253644845c4311baeb7d3e681a0c9091803440c5ecc0980494fbfc8.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to the **nearest tenth**, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is{{field-1}}.\n\nEnter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point if needed.\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The marked angles are rounded to the precision shown; calculate the area estimate using the marked values." :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/b4/b4b6aacce80889f8d867015f7f9c4400acba3d51fced00264062968c8295204a.png)\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9.5\\cdot 10.3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{120.7}^{∘} \\\\  & =48.925\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{120.7}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈42.068\\,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 13.4\\cdot 14.4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{76.4}^{∘} \\\\  & =96.48\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{76.4}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈93.774\\,8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & ≈42.068\\,3+93.774\\,8 \\\\  & ≈135.843\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the **nearest tenth**, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $135.8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556097 :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a611645078047bb51f06d090a3be1b3628045067b106354fa62a167f7737c10.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to the **nearest tenth**, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is{{field-1}}.\n\nEnter the rounded number using digits and a decimal point if needed.\n\nThe diagram is not drawn to scale. Use the marked measurements. The marked angles are rounded to the precision shown; calculate the area estimate using the marked values." :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $△ABD$ and $△BDC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/cc/ccae61d17dd53252c3c08c885d92c7cc7444f97a339feed8db2e7568883e54ce.png)\n\nFirst we find the area for $△ABD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 19.3\\cdot 18.7\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{94.1}^{∘} \\\\  & =180.455\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{94.1}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈179.993\\,2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $△BCD:$ \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{BDC} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 11.6\\cdot 29.3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{71.2}^{∘} \\\\  & =169.94\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{71.2}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈160.873\\,6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABD}+{A}_{BDC} \\\\  & ≈179.993\\,2+160.873\\,6 \\\\  & ≈340.866\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the **nearest tenth**, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $340.9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556105 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(-2)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =(-2)(-2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =4 x + 8 \\\\ z & =4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4 x + 8$ (or $4 x - z + 8=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨4,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i - 4 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-4⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-4⟩)=⟨1,0,4⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556111 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(3)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =3 x + 6 \\\\ z & =3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 x + 2$ (or $3 x - z + 2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i - 3 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-3⟩)=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556113 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(-2)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =(-2)(3)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =18 - 6 y \\\\ z & =15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=15 - 6 y$ (or $- 6 y - z + 15=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j - 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-6⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556118 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,3,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =6 - 2 y \\\\ z & =3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 - 2 y$ (or $- 2 y - z + 3=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j - 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556132 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=8$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(8)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =8 y - 16 \\\\ z & =8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=8 y - 19$ (or $8 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,8,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,8,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 8 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,8⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556140 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Given that the point $P(-3,3,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-3,3) & ={f}_{y}(-3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(2)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =2 y - 6 \\\\ z & =2 y - 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-3,3,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=-3 \\\\ z=2 y - 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2 y - 7$ (or $2 y - z - 7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,2⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556144 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,-3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(8)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =(8)(2)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(16)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =16 y - 32 \\\\ z & =16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=16 y - 35$ (or $16 y - z - 35=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,16,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,16,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 16 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,16⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556160 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=4.$ Given that the point $P(-2,4,-4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(3)x$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =(3)(-2)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(-6)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =- 6 x - 12 \\\\ z & =- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 6 x - 16$ (or $- 6 x - z - 16=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,6⟩)=⟨1,0,-6⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556162 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =- 2 x - 4 \\\\ z & =- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 2 x - 4$ (or $- 2 x - z - 4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i + 2 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,2⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,2⟩)=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556166 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Given that the point $P(-3,3,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=(2)y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,3) & =(2)(3)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-3,3) & ={f}_{y}(-3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =6 y - 18 \\\\ z & =6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-3,3,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=-3 \\\\ z=6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6 y - 19$ (or $6 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,6⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556173 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle 4 x - \\frac{1}{z},\\,4 y + \\frac{2}{z},\\,3 z\\right\\rangle,\\quad r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u e^{v},\\,u e^{- v},\\,u\\right\\rangle,\\quad (u,v)\\in D\\subset\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nAssume $D\\subset\\{(u,v):u>0\\}$, so the vector field is defined on $S$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_S F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u e^{v}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u e^{- v}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle e^{v},\\,e^{- v},\\,1\\right\\rangle \\\\ r'_v & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u e^{v}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u e^{- v}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle u e^{v},\\,- u e^{- v},\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u\\times r'_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\e^{v}&e^{- v}&1\\\\u e^{v}&- u e^{- v}&0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}e^{- v}&1\\\\- u e^{- v}&0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}e^{v}&1\\\\u e^{v}&0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}e^{v}&e^{- v}\\\\u e^{v}&- u e^{- v}\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle u e^{- v},\\,u e^{v},\\,- 2 u\\right\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =\\left\\langle 4 x - \\frac{1}{z},\\,4 y + \\frac{2}{z},\\,3 z\\right\\rangle\\big|_{x=u e^{v},\\,y=u e^{- v},\\,z=u} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 4 \\left(u e^{v}\\right) - \\frac{1}{\\left(u\\right)},\\,4 \\left(u e^{- v}\\right) + \\frac{2}{\\left(u\\right)},\\,3 \\left(u\\right)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 4 u e^{v} - \\frac{1}{u},\\,4 u e^{- v} + \\frac{2}{u},\\,3 u\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v) & =\\left\\langle 4 u e^{v} - \\frac{1}{u},\\,4 u e^{- v} + \\frac{2}{u},\\,3 u\\right\\rangle\\cdot \\left\\langle u e^{- v},\\,u e^{v},\\,- 2 u\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left(4 u e^{v} - \\frac{1}{u}\\right)\\cdot\\left(u e^{- v}\\right)+\\left(4 u e^{- v} + \\frac{2}{u}\\right)\\cdot\\left(u e^{v}\\right)+\\left(3 u\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 2 u\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(4 u^{2} - e^{- v}\\right)+\\left(4 u^{2} + 2 e^{v}\\right)+\\left(- 6 u^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2 u^{2} + 2 e^{v} - e^{- v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S & =\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot(r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  & =\\iint_D 2 u^{2} + 2 e^{v} - e^{- v}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556184 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle x - \\frac{3}{z},\\,3 y + \\frac{4}{z},\\,z\\right\\rangle,\\quad r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u e^{v},\\,u e^{- v},\\,u\\right\\rangle,\\quad (u,v)\\in D\\subset\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nAssume $D\\subset\\{(u,v):u>0\\}$, so the vector field is defined on $S$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_S F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u e^{v}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u e^{- v}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle e^{v},\\,e^{- v},\\,1\\right\\rangle \\\\ r'_v & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u e^{v}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u e^{- v}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle u e^{v},\\,- u e^{- v},\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u\\times r'_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\e^{v}&e^{- v}&1\\\\u e^{v}&- u e^{- v}&0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}e^{- v}&1\\\\- u e^{- v}&0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}e^{v}&1\\\\u e^{v}&0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}e^{v}&e^{- v}\\\\u e^{v}&- u e^{- v}\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle u e^{- v},\\,u e^{v},\\,- 2 u\\right\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =\\left\\langle x - \\frac{3}{z},\\,3 y + \\frac{4}{z},\\,z\\right\\rangle\\big|_{x=u e^{v},\\,y=u e^{- v},\\,z=u} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\left(u e^{v}\\right) - \\frac{3}{\\left(u\\right)},\\,3 \\left(u e^{- v}\\right) + \\frac{4}{\\left(u\\right)},\\,\\left(u\\right)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle u e^{v} - \\frac{3}{u},\\,3 u e^{- v} + \\frac{4}{u},\\,u\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v) & =\\left\\langle u e^{v} - \\frac{3}{u},\\,3 u e^{- v} + \\frac{4}{u},\\,u\\right\\rangle\\cdot \\left\\langle u e^{- v},\\,u e^{v},\\,- 2 u\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left(u e^{v} - \\frac{3}{u}\\right)\\cdot\\left(u e^{- v}\\right)+\\left(3 u e^{- v} + \\frac{4}{u}\\right)\\cdot\\left(u e^{v}\\right)+\\left(u\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 2 u\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(u^{2} - 3 e^{- v}\\right)+\\left(3 u^{2} + 4 e^{v}\\right)+\\left(- 2 u^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2 u^{2} + 4 e^{v} - 3 e^{- v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S & =\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot(r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  & =\\iint_D 2 u^{2} + 4 e^{v} - 3 e^{- v}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556187 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle 3 x,\\,2 y,\\,z\\right\\rangle,\\quad r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,3 u^{2}\\right\\rangle,\\quad (u,v)\\in D\\subset\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nAssume $D\\subset\\{(u,v):u>0,\\ 0<v<2\\pi\\}$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_S F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(3 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,\\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,6 u\\right\\rangle \\\\ r'_v & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(3 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u\\times r'_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&6 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&6 u\\\\u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&6 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - 6 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 6 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =\\left\\langle 3 x,\\,2 y,\\,z\\right\\rangle\\big|_{x=u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,y=u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,z=3 u^{2}} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 3 \\left(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,2 \\left(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,\\left(3 u^{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,3 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v) & =\\left\\langle 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,3 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\cdot \\left\\langle - 6 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 6 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left(3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 6 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 6 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 u^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(u\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(18 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 18 u^{3}\\right)+\\left(- 12 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 u^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 15 u^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S & =\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot(r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  & =\\iint_D 6 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 15 u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556190 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle 3 x,\\,y,\\,3 z\\right\\rangle,\\quad r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,2 u^{2}\\right\\rangle,\\quad (u,v)\\in D\\subset\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nAssume $D\\subset\\{(u,v):u>0,\\ 0<v<2\\pi\\}$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_S F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(2 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,\\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,4 u\\right\\rangle \\\\ r'_v & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(2 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u\\times r'_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&4 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&4 u\\\\u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&4 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - 4 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 4 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =\\left\\langle 3 x,\\,y,\\,3 z\\right\\rangle\\big|_{x=u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,y=u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,z=2 u^{2}} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 3 \\left(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,\\left(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,3 \\left(2 u^{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,6 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v) & =\\left\\langle 3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,6 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\cdot \\left\\langle - 4 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 4 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left(3 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 4 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 4 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(6 u^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(u\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(12 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 12 u^{3}\\right)+\\left(- 4 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(6 u^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 6 u^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S & =\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot(r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  & =\\iint_D 8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 6 u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556207 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=\\left\\langle 2 x,\\,3 y,\\,3 z\\right\\rangle,\\quad r(u,v)=\\left\\langle u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,4 u^{2}\\right\\rangle,\\quad (u,v)\\in D\\subset\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nAssume $D\\subset\\{(u,v):u>0,\\ 0<v<2\\pi\\}$.\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_D \\boxed{}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S=\\iint_S F\\cdot n\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial u}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(4 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,\\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,8 u\\right\\rangle \\\\ r'_v & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v},\\,\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial v}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}),\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(4 u^{2})\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,0\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'_u\\times r'_v & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&8 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&8 u\\\\u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},-\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&8 u\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\\\- u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle - 8 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =\\left\\langle 2 x,\\,3 y,\\,3 z\\right\\rangle\\big|_{x=u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,y=u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,z=4 u^{2}} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2 \\left(u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,3 \\left(u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right),\\,3 \\left(4 u^{2}\\right)\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left\\langle 2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,12 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot (r'_u\\times r'_v) & =\\left\\langle 2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,12 u^{2}\\right\\rangle\\cdot \\left\\langle - 8 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)},\\,- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)},\\,u\\right\\rangle \\\\  & =\\left(2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 8 u^{2} \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(3 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 8 u^{2} \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(12 u^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(u\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(16 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 16 u^{3}\\right)+\\left(- 24 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)+\\left(12 u^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =- 8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 4 u^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf S & =\\iint_D F(r(u,v))\\cdot(r'_u\\times r'_v)\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\  & =\\iint_D - 8 u^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(v \\right)} - 4 u^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556215 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(3\\sin \\theta-6\\cos \\theta).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(3\\sin \\theta-6\\cos \\theta) = 4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\sin^{2}\\theta+3\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n4\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\sin^{2}\\theta+3\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6\\cos^{2}\\theta = 7\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n7\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-6(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 7 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(4\\cos \\theta-6\\sin \\theta)+\\cos \\theta(3\\sin \\theta-6\\cos \\theta) = 7 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556225 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\sin \\theta(5\\cos \\theta-4\\sin \\theta)-4\\cos^{2}\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(5\\cos \\theta-4\\sin \\theta)-4\\cos^{2}\\theta = 5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4\\sin^{2}\\theta-4\\cos^{2}\\theta\n$$\n\nNext, we group the $\\sin^2 \\theta$ and $\\cos^2 \\theta$ terms.\n\n$$\n5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4\\sin^{2}\\theta-4\\cos^{2}\\theta = 5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta)\n$$\n\nThen, we use $\\sin^2{\\theta}+\\cos^2{\\theta}=1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n5\\sin \\theta\\cos \\theta-4(\\sin^{2}\\theta+\\cos^{2}\\theta) = 5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\sin \\theta(5\\cos \\theta-4\\sin \\theta)-4\\cos^{2}\\theta = 5 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - 4.\n$$"}]}
